From 1b9767e93d8b0fc46b3f1f556da26892a4893495 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ermete Architect <esenese@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:02:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add GREENBOOT_AUTO_REBOOT to roll back without
 rebooting

Some systems must never reboot on their own, for example a desktop where the
user decides when the machine restarts. Today a failed required health check
always ends in `systemctl reboot`, and greenboot.conf has no way to stop it.

Add GREENBOOT_AUTO_REBOOT (default true, so nothing changes unless it is set).
When it is false and a required health check fails, greenboot still runs the
red.d scripts and sets boot_success=0, and then, if the booted deployment is
a new one (greenboot_next_deployment_id is set), it rolls back right away:
the same `bootc rollback` / `rpm-ostree rollback` and the same cleanup of
boot_counter and greenboot_next_deployment_id as when the boot counter runs
out, without the reboot. The previous deployment becomes the default, so the
next boot, whenever it is started, is the previous deployment, and greenboot
on that boot finds "Rollback successful" in the previous boot's journal and
reports the fallback, as it does today. GREENBOOT_MAX_BOOT_ATTEMPTS is not
used in this mode. Without a rollback trigger greenboot asks for manual
intervention, as it does when the counter runs out.

The rollback does not go through GRUB's boot counter. With the counter, the
fallback happens only if boot_success is still 0 when GRUB runs
(08_greenboot.cfg, and Fedora's 08_fallback_counting), but between the
failure and a reboot the user starts, anything may set boot_success=1:
grub-boot-success.timer does so after two minutes of a user session, through
grub2-set-bootflag, which does not look at boot_counter. The next boot would
then be the failed deployment again.

The MOTD says that the previous deployment will boot at the next restart.
